\section{Why the result is resampled rather than joined}{

The prediction grid is not a complete lattice — it is the ocean-only subset
of one, and 2.77 per cent of Atl IMRP's cells do not sit on the 10 km lattice
at all. \code{dsmextra} therefore rasterises it onto a lattice of its own, whose
origin is offset from the prediction raster's by half a cell. Joining on
coordinates silently matches nothing. This function resamples
(\code{method = "near"}) onto \code{pred.raster} instead, so every returned row is a
cell of the surface being assessed. Cells \code{dsmextra} could not place come
back \code{NA} and are counted in the return value rather than guessed at.
}

\section{Running before the predictions exist}{

The assessment itself needs only the segments and the prediction grid, so it
is run \strong{before} \verb{03.00_Do_all_prediction.Rmd} - its findings are an input to
masking, not a commentary on predictions already made. \code{pred.raster} is
therefore optional. When it is \code{NULL} the target geometry is rasterized from
\code{predgrid} by \code{\link[=make.raster]{make.raster()}}, which is the same template
\code{\link[=make.season.raster]{make.season.raster()}} builds when the predictions are written, so the \code{cell}
indices in the returned \code{cells} tibble address the prediction rasters that
\code{03.00} will later write. Verified on Atl IMRP ATPU Fall: identical
resolution, extent, CRS and 222 x 154 dimensions, 12,970 non-\code{NA} cells on
each, and no cell non-\code{NA} in one but not the other.

Passing a \code{pred.raster} explicitly is still supported and gives the same
answer; it is worth doing only when assessing a surface that is not on the
prediction grid's geometry.
}

\section{Maps}{

Maps are \strong{not} generated here. \code{extrapolation_analysis(map.generate = TRUE)} calls \code{print()} on each leaflet widget, which is the wrong behaviour
inside a function and drops the widgets on the floor when it is called from
a loop. The report calls \code{\link[dsmextra:map_extrapolation]{dsmextra::map_extrapolation()}} itself on
\verb{$extrap$extrapolation} and \verb{$extrap$nearby}, where it can pass the sightings
and tracks overlays and control display.
}

